>> title: Matzoh Ball Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: chicken, matzo, dumpling, stovetop, deli
>> counters: Deli
>> aka: matzo ball soup, matzah ball soup, matza ball soup, knaidlach, kneidlach, jewish penicillin, chicken soup with matzo balls
>> pairs-with: chicken-broth, schmaltz
>> servings: 6
>> time: 1 hr 50 min
>> slack: narrow — the hour in the fridge is not optional: the meal has to drink the liquid or the balls fall apart in the pot
>> step.1: whisk the eggs with schmaltz and seltzer
>> step.2: stir in the matzo meal, chill 1 hr
>> step.3: shape wet, poach 40 min covered
>> step.4: simmer the broth 20 min with carrot and dill
>> step.5: ladle the broth over the balls

Whisk @eggs{4%large} with @schmaltz{1/4%cup}(60 mL, melted and cooled), @seltzer{1/4%cup}(60 mL), @kosher salt{1 1/4%tsp} and @white pepper{1/4%tsp} until slack and foamy. These are floaters: schmaltz and seltzer both hold air, and the whole argument at this counter is between people who want a cloud and people who want a sinker they can cut with a spoon.

Stir @matzo meal{1%cup}(120 g) and @fresh dill{2%Tbs}(chopped) into @&(~1)egg mixture{} just until no dry meal is left, then ~chill{1%hr}. Overworking it now makes them tough, and the hour in the fridge is not optional — the meal has to drink the liquid or the balls fall apart in the pot.

Shape @&(~1)matzo batter{} into twelve loose balls with wet hands, drop them into @water{4%qt}(3.8 L, boiling) with @kosher salt{1%Tbs}, cover the pot and ~poach{40%min} without lifting the lid. They cook in salted water rather than in the soup, because a pot of matzo balls leaves behind enough starch to turn good broth to fog.

Simmer @clear chicken broth{2%qt}(1.9 L) for ~simmer{20%min} with @carrots{2}(sliced into coins), @fresh dill{2%sprigs} and @kosher salt{1%tsp}, until the carrot is tender and the broth tastes seasoned on its own.

Ladle @&(~1)hot broth{} over @&(~2)matzo balls{}, two to a bowl, with a coin or three of carrot and a little more dill. Lift the balls straight from their pot to the bowl; anything left standing in the cooking water goes waterlogged in about ten minutes.
